Ladybird now recascades the inherited font size wherever the family
changes between monospace and any other, against that family’s
medium size. Blink rescales a size that derives from a keyword in
FontBuilder::CheckForGenericFamilyChange(), keeping the keyword
in its FontDescription; WebKit does the same in BuilderCustom::
applyValueFontFamily(), through useFixedDefaultSize(); Gecko carries
the keyword in the computed font size’s keyword_info, and recomputes
it in recompute_keyword_font_size_if_needed(). In editing, Blink’s
LegacyFontSizeFromCSSValue() and WebKit’s legacyFontSizeFromCSSValue()
take the same monospace flag. Run in Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and
WebKit 26.5, all 48 cases of the test match Chrome and WebKit; Firefox
differs on “font-size: initial” in monospace (16px) and on a zoomed
one. For a calc() font size, Ladybird follows Blink and WebKit: One
made of a percentage alone carries the keyword through, as Blink’s
StyleBuilderConverterBase::ConvertFontSize() does for a math function
whose type is a percentage, so calc(110%) in monospace is 14.3px; any
other is absolute, so calc(110% + 0px) and calc(1.1em) are 17.6px,
and calc(1em + 2px) 18px. Gecko treats every calc() as absolute, and
gives 17.6px for calc(110%). Within the recascade, Blink and WebKit
resolve an ancestor’s ex or ch size against its parent’s family
at the recascaded size, as Ladybird now does; Gecko resolves it
at the size the parent has.
